// Plain words for this work, by passage key. See ./index.ts.

export const PLAIN_STOBAEUS: Record<string, string> = {
  "herm.sh.ex1.1": "Hermes says that, out of love for humankind and devotion to God, he is taking up the pen for the first time, to teach his son. There is no truer devotion than to know the things that exist and to thank their Maker for them, and he will never stop doing that.",
  "herm.sh.ex1.2": "Tat asks how anyone can live wisely if nothing down here is true. Hermes answers: be devout. Devotion leads to the height of philosophy, and without philosophy the height of devotion cannot be reached. Whoever learns what exists, how it is ordered, by whom and for whose sake, will thank the Maker for everything, as a good father, an excellent nurse and a steward who never betrays his trust.",
  "herm.sh.ex1.3": "Hermes says that whoever gives thanks will be devout, and the devout will come to know where Truth is and what it is, and grow more devout as they learn. A soul in a body that has once leapt up and taken hold of the true Good can never slip back into its opposite. Once the soul knows the source of its peace, it is filled with wonderful love, forgets every evil, and can no longer keep away from the Good.",
  "herm.sh.ex1.4": "Hermes tells his son this is the goal of devotion. Reach it and he will live nobly and die happily, because his soul will know where to fly back to. This is the only way, the path to Truth that their ancestors also walked and on which they found the Good. The path is holy and smooth, but hard for a soul to walk while it is still in a body.",
  "herm.sh.ex1.5": "Hermes says the soul must first fight against itself, stir up a great split inside, and make sure one part of it wins. There is a contest of the one against the two: the one tries to flee upward, the two drag it down. The struggle between them is fierce, the one wanting to escape, the others trying to hold it back.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- the 'one' and the 'two' are the rational part of the soul against its two irrational parts (Mead's usual reading)\n- the 'one' is the soul and the 'two' are other forces pulling at it; the text does not name them",
  "herm.sh.ex1.6": "Hermes says the two sides do not win in the same way. The one hurries up toward the Good, the others sink toward the bad; the one longs to be free, the others love their slavery. If the two are beaten, they lose their own selves and their ruler; if the one is beaten, it is chased about and tortured by life down here. This one, he tells his son, is the guide on the path to the other world. He must leave his body behind before its end comes, win the life of struggle, and so go home as the victor.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'leave the body behind' means to turn away from the body's claims while still alive\n- it means that the soul must be freed from the body before death in some more literal, ecstatic sense",
  "herm.sh.ex1.7": "Hermes says he will now run through things in short headings, so that Tat can remember what he has heard at length. Everything that exists is in motion; only what does not exist is free of it. Every body changes, but not every body can be dissolved. Not every living thing is mortal, nor every one immortal. What can be dissolved can be destroyed; what lasts is what does not change, and what does not change is eternal. What is always coming into being is always being destroyed; what comes into being once for all is never destroyed and never becomes something else.",
  "herm.sh.ex1.8": "More short headings: God is first, the Cosmos second, man third. The Cosmos exists for man's sake, and man for God's. The irrational part of the soul is mortal; the rational part is immortal. All true being (essence) is immortal and unchanging. Everything that exists is twofold; nothing that exists stays put. Not everything is moved by a soul, but the soul moves everything that exists.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'naught of existing things remains' means nothing in existence stays as it is\n- it means nothing exists that is not twofold",
  "herm.sh.ex1.9": "More headings: everything that is acted on can be sensed, but not everything that can be sensed is acted on. Whatever feels pain also feels pleasure, and that is a mortal life; a life that feels pleasure but no pain is an immortal one. Not every body is subject to disease, but every body subject to disease is also subject to being dissolved.",
  "herm.sh.ex1.10": "More headings: Mind is in God, reasoning is in man. Reason is in the mind, and the mind is above all suffering. Nothing in a body is true; everything bodiless is free from untruth. Everything that comes to be can change, but not everything that comes to be must be dissolved. Nothing on earth is good, and nothing in heaven is bad.",
  "herm.sh.ex1.11": "More headings: God is good and man is bad. Good acts freely; bad acts against the will. The gods choose good things because they are good. The good law of the mighty One is the good law, and good law is the law itself. Time belongs to the gods, law to men. Badness is the stuff the world feeds on; time is what brings man to his end.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'time's for the gods' means the gods live in endless time while men live under law\n- it means the gods rule over time as men are ruled by law",
  "herm.sh.ex1.12": "More headings: everything in heaven is unchanging; everything on earth changes. Nothing in heaven is a slave and nothing on earth is free. Nothing in heaven is unknowable, and nothing on earth can truly be known. The things of earth have no fellowship with the things of heaven. Everything in heaven is blameless, everything on earth blameworthy. The immortal is not mortal, nor the mortal immortal. What is sown does not always come to birth, but what is born must always have been sown.",
  "herm.sh.ex1.13": "More headings: a body that can be dissolved has two periods, from conception to birth and from birth to death; an everlasting body has only the time since its birth. Things that can be dissolved grow and shrink. Matter that is dissolved goes through two opposite changes, death and birth, while everlasting matter changes only into itself or into things like itself. A man's birth is the start of his dissolution, and his dissolution the start of his birth. What departs returns, and what returns departs again.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'dissolution the beginning of his birth' refers to rebirth of the soul in another body\n- it refers only to the matter of the body passing into new births",
  "herm.sh.ex1.14": "More headings: of the things that exist, some are in bodies, some in forms and some in activities. Body is in forms, and form and activity are in body. The deathless has no share in the mortal, but the mortal has a share in the deathless. A mortal body does not rise into the deathless one; the deathless one comes down into the mortal frame. Activities do not rise; they come down.",
  "herm.sh.ex1.15": "More headings: the things of earth give nothing to the things of heaven, but heaven pours every benefit on earth. Heaven holds the everlasting bodies, earth the perishable ones. Earth is without reason, heaven has reason. Heaven is the first element, earth the last. Foreknowledge is God's order, and Necessity is Foreknowledge's servant. Fortune is the path of what is disorderly, an image of activity, a false opinion. What is God? The unchangeable Good. What is man? The bad that can change.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'the things in heaven are under it; the things on earth above the earth' means heavenly things are governed by heaven while earthly things lie on the earth's surface\n- the line is corrupt and its sense is uncertain",
  "herm.sh.ex1.16": "Hermes says that if Tat remembers these headings, he will also remember the longer teaching, since these are its summary. But he should avoid discussing them with the crowd, not to keep them to himself selfishly, but so as not to look ridiculous to them. Like accepts like; unlike is never friend to unlike. Very few will listen to such words, maybe not even those few. These words also have a strange power of their own: they push the wicked further into wickedness. So Tat should protect the many from them, because they do not understand the power of what has been said.",
  "herm.sh.ex1.17": "Tat asks what he means. Hermes explains: the animal part of a human leans toward the bad and lives with it because it loves it. If this animal learned that the Cosmos is subject to coming-to-be and that everything comes and goes by Foreknowledge and Necessity, with Fate ruling all, it would soon grow worse. It would despise the whole universe as something that merely comes to be, blame Fate for every wrong, and never stop doing evil. So such people should be looked after: left in ignorance, they will be less bad out of fear of the unknown.",
  "herm.sh.ex2": "Hermes says that to understand God is hard and to speak of him impossible. The bodiless cannot be expressed through a body, the perfect cannot be grasped by the imperfect, and it is hard for the eternal to keep company with the short-lived. The one lasts for ever, the other passes; the one is in the clear light of Truth, the other in the shadow of appearance. The weaker is as far from the stronger, and the lesser from the greater, as the mortal is from the divine, and this distance dims the vision of the Beautiful. Bodies are seen with eyes and spoken of with tongues, but what has no body, is unseen and without shape, and is not made of matter cannot be grasped by our senses. Hermes says he has it in mind that God is what cannot be spoken of.",
  "herm.sh.ex3.1": "Hermes tells Tat that a human being cannot dare to speak about Truth, because a human is an imperfect creature, made of imperfect parts, his body patched together from many alien bodies. What he can rightly say is that Truth exists only in the eternal bodies, which are true in themselves: fire that is pure fire, earth pure earth, air pure air, water pure water. Our bodies are mixtures of all these, and are none of them truly. Since our make-up did not begin from Truth, how can it see or speak Truth? It can only have an idea of it, and only if God wills.",
  "herm.sh.ex3.2": "Hermes says that nothing on earth is Truth; things here are only copies of the True, and only a few of them are even that. The rest are falsehood and error, mere appearances like images. When an appearance receives the influence from above, it becomes a copy of the Truth; without that energy from above it stays false. A portrait shows a body but is not one: it seems to have eyes but sees nothing, hears nothing, and tricks the viewers into thinking they see something true. If we grasp or see each thing as it really is, we truly grasp and see; if we see things otherwise than they are, we know nothing true.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'All, then, who do not see what's false see truth' means those who are not deceived by appearances see truly\n- the sentence may be corrupt in Stobaeus and its exact sense is uncertain",
  "herm.sh.ex3.3": "Tat asks whether there is Truth on earth after all. Hermes says he is quite wrong: Truth is nowhere on earth and cannot be. But some people can have an idea of it, if God grants them the power of divine vision. Nothing on earth is true; everything here is appearance and show. Yet knowing and saying true things should not, surely, be called the Truth itself.",
  "herm.sh.ex3.4": "Tat asks how we can know and speak of true things when nothing on earth is true. Hermes says the one true thing is that we know nothing true down here. Truth is the most perfect virtue, the highest Good, untroubled by matter and unbounded by body: bare, evident, changeless, majestic, unalterable Good. But things down here cannot receive this Good: they perish, suffer, dissolve, change and keep altering, born from one another. Things that are not even true to themselves cannot be true, for whatever alters is untrue: it does not stay as it is but shows us one appearance after another.",
  "herm.sh.ex3.5": "Tat asks whether even man is not true. Hermes says that as man he is not, because what is true is made from itself alone and stays as it is, while man is made of many things and does not stay himself. He changes from age to age and form to form, even while in the same body. Many people fail to recognise their children after a short time apart, and children their parents. Can something that changes until it is unrecognisable be true? Isn't it rather false, coming and going in endless changing shows? A true thing is one that stays and lasts for ever. 'Man' does not last for ever, so is not true; 'man' is an appearance, and appearance is the height of untruth.",
  "herm.sh.ex3.6": "Tat asks whether the heavenly bodies outside us, which also change, are not true. Hermes says that whatever is born and changes is not truly true, but because they are made by the Forefather of all, their matter is true; still, in changing they have something false in them. Tat asks whether the Sun alone, which changes least, is Truth. Hermes says no; but the Sun alone has been entrusted with making all things in the cosmos, ruling and making all, and so Hermes honours him, worships his Truth and recognises him as the Maker after the One and First. The first Truth, he says, is the One and Only: not of matter or in a body, colourless, shapeless, changeless, always existing. What is untrue perishes, but the forethought of the True embraces all perishing things on earth. There is no birth without decay, and decay follows every birth so that things may be born again; what is born comes from what is destroyed, and must itself be destroyed, so that coming-to-be never stops. So Tat should recognise the Sun as the Maker of the birth and death of all things.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'him' in the last sentence is the Sun, the Demiurge just named\n- 'him' is the Forefather or the first Truth",
  "herm.sh.ex3.8": "Hermes says that things born out of destruction must be false, because they keep turning into one thing and then another and can never become the same. What is not the same cannot be true. So they should be called appearances: a man should properly be called a man's appearance, and so with a child, a youth, a grown man and an old man. None of them stays what it is named. Because they change, both earlier and present things are untrue. But Tat should think of even these untruths as activities hanging down from Truth above; and so, Hermes says, untruth is the working of Truth.",
  "herm.sh.ex4.1": "Hermes says there is something that goes beyond being, above all existing things and all that truly are. Because of it there is the general being that is common to the things known by the mind and to the beings understood by the rule of sameness. Those that fall under the opposite rule, of otherness, exist again in their own right. Nature is a being that the senses can perceive, which holds all things that can be sensed.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'those which are contrary to these' are the things of sense, which exist by difference rather than sameness\n- the sentence is compressed in Stobaeus and its exact reference is unclear",
  "herm.sh.ex4.2": "Hermes says that between these stand the gods known by the mind and the gods perceived by the senses. What belongs to understanding shares in the gods known only by the mind; what belongs to opinion has its part with the gods of sense. The sense-perceived gods are images of the mind-known ones: the Sun, for example, is the image of the Maker-God above Heaven. As that God made the universe, so the Sun makes the animals, produces plants and governs the breaths.",
  "herm.sh.ex5": "Hermes says Matter has been born and has also existed before it came into existence. Matter is the vessel of coming-to-be, and coming-to-be is how God acts, God who has no need to come to be and exists before all. Receiving the seed of coming-to-be, Matter herself was born, became subject to change and took shapes; contriving the forms of her own changes, she presided over her own changing. Matter's unborn state was formlessness; her birth is her being set into activity.",
  "herm.sh.ex6.1": "The excerpt asks about the three times, past, present and future. They do not exist on their own, nor are they simply one; and yet they are one, and also each on its own. If you think the present exists without the past, it cannot be present unless it has already become past. The present comes from the past, and the future comes from the present. But to look more closely, the argument runs as follows.",
  "herm.sh.ex6.2": "Past time passes into no longer being; future time does not exist because it is not present; even the present is not present, since it keeps going on. Time never stands still but is always turning, with no point at which it stops: so how can it be called an 'instant', when it cannot stand at all? Yet past joins present and present joins future, so they are one, never apart in their sameness, oneness and continuity. Time is therefore both continuous and discontinuous, while remaining one and the same. (Mead adds three Greek words in brackets here, which are not reproduced.)",
  "herm.sh.ex7.1": "Hermes tells Tat that the Lord and Maker of all eternal bodies made them once and for all and makes no more of them. He left them to themselves, joined to one another, lacking nothing, as everlasting things. If they need anything, it is each other, not any increase in their number from outside, since they are immortal. Bodies made by him must be of this kind.",
  "herm.sh.ex7.2": "Hermes says our maker, however, is himself in a body. He made us and makes, and always will make, bodies that decay and die. It would not be right for him to imitate his own Maker, and it is impossible anyway, because that Maker created out of the first, bodiless essence, while ours made us out of the bodily stuff his Lord had already brought into being.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'our Demiurge' who is in a body is the Sun (Mead's note suggests this)\n- it is some other lower maker-god; the excerpt does not name him",
  "herm.sh.ex7.3": "Hermes says it follows that those eternal bodies, made from bodiless essence, do not die, while ours decay and die, because our matter is made of bodies, as their weakness and need of help shows. Our bodies could not hold together unless they were fed from similar elements and renewed every day. A stream of earth, water, fire and air flows into us, renewing our bodies and holding our tent together. We are too weak to bear our own movements even for one day: if our bodies did not rest at night, we would not last a day.",
  "herm.sh.ex7.4": "Hermes says that our Maker, being good and foreseeing everything, gave sleep so that the living creature might last: the greatest relief from the tiredness of movement, with equal time or more set aside for rest. Sleep's power is the opposite of the soul's but no less: as the soul is the energy of movement, so bodies cannot live without sleep. It relaxes and restores the limbs, and inside it turns the new matter flowing in into body, sending each element to its place: water to the blood, earth to bones and marrow, air to nerves and veins, fire to the eyes. That is why the body takes such delight in sleep.",
  "herm.sh.ex8.1": "Tat thanks his father and asks for more. Hermes had said that knowledge and skill are the activity of the rational, and that irrational creatures are called irrational because they lack reason. By that argument, Tat says, irrational creatures must have no share in knowledge or skill, because they lack reason.",
  "herm.sh.ex8.2": "Hermes agrees that this follows. Tat asks how, then, some irrational creatures seem to use intelligence and skill: ants store food for winter, birds build nests, four-footed animals know their own dens. Hermes says they do these things not by knowledge or skill but by nature. Knowledge and skill are taught, and none of these creatures is taught anything. What nature does comes from the general activity of things; what skill and knowledge do is done by those who know, not by everyone; what everyone does is set going by nature.",
  "herm.sh.ex8.3": "Hermes gives an example: everyone looks up at the sky, but not everyone is a musician, an archer or a hunter; some have learned one thing and others another, with knowledge and skill at work in them. Likewise, if only some ants stored food and others did not, Tat could rightly say they acted by knowledge and skill. But since all of them are driven to it by nature, even against their will, they plainly do it neither by knowledge nor by skill.",
  "herm.sh.ex8.4": "Hermes says these activities (energies), though bodiless in themselves, are found in bodies and act through bodies. Because they are bodiless, Tat calls them immortal; but because they cannot act without bodies, Hermes says they are always in a body. Things brought into being for a purpose, under Providence and Fate, can never stop doing their proper work. What is will always be, because being is the very body and life of it.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'that this [being] is [the very] body and the life of it' means being is what gives such things body and life\n- the clause is obscure in Stobaeus's Greek and Mead fills it out with his own words in brackets",
  "herm.sh.ex8.5": "Hermes says it follows that these are always bodies, and that the making of bodies is itself an eternal activity. Bodies on earth can be dissolved, but there must always be bodies on earth to serve as places and tools for the activities. The activities themselves are immortal, and what is immortal exists for ever: so body-making, if it exists at all, is an activity.",
  "herm.sh.ex8.6": "Hermes says the activities go with the soul, but do not all appear at once. Some work in a person from birth, joined to the soul's irrational parts; the purer ones, as he grows older, work with the soul's rational part. All of them depend on bodies: these body-making activities come down from divine bodies into mortal ones, and each is always active, either around the body or around the soul, even with the soul when it has no body. The soul is not always in a mortal body, since it can exist without one, but the activities can never exist without bodies. He gives a sacred saying: body cannot last without soul, but its being can.",
  "herm.sh.ex8.7": "Tat asks what this means. Hermes says that when the soul leaves the body, the body remains, and while it remains it is still active, being broken up and made to disappear; a body without activity could not undergo this. So the activity stays with the body after the soul has gone. The difference between an immortal body and a mortal one is that the immortal one is made of a single matter, and the mortal one is not. The first acts and the second is acted on. What makes active is stronger, and what is made active is weaker; the stronger, free and in command, leads, and the weaker follows like a slave.",
  "herm.sh.ex8.8": "Hermes says the activities work not only in bodies with souls but in bodies without souls too, such as wood and stones: making them grow, bear fruit and ripen, and also dissolving, melting, rotting and crumbling them, and causing every change that soulless bodies can undergo. 'Activity' is the name for what is happening, for coming-to-be. Many things, indeed all things, must always be coming to be; the Cosmos is never without any of the things that exist, and, carried along in itself for ever, it carries things that will never stop being destroyed again.",
  "herm.sh.ex8.9": "Hermes says every activity, whatever it is and in whatever body, is deathless. Some activities belong to divine bodies and some to perishable ones; some are general and some particular; some belong to whole classes and some to the parts of each class. The divine ones act through everlasting bodies and are perfect because those bodies are perfect. Partial ones act through each single living thing, and particular ones through each single existing thing.",
  "herm.sh.ex8.10": "Hermes says the argument shows that all things are full of activities. Activities have to be in bodies, and there are many bodies in the Cosmos, but the activities are far more numerous than the bodies. Often one body has a first, a second and a third activity, besides the general ones that come with it. By general ones he means the purely bodily ones that work through the senses and movements of the body, without which an animal's body cannot survive.",
  "herm.sh.ex8.11": "Hermes says human souls have a second class of activities, the particular ones that work through arts, sciences, practices and deliberate actions. Feelings follow on activities, or rather complete them. He then explains the difference between activity and sensation: activity is sent down from above, while sensation, which is in the body and comes from it, receives the activity and makes it visible, as if giving it a body. So sensations are bodily and mortal, and last only as long as the body; they are born with the body and die with it.",
  "herm.sh.ex8.12": "Hermes says immortal bodies have no sensation in themselves, as if they were made of some special essence. Sensation arises only from something bad or good being added to a body or taken away from it, and with eternal bodies nothing is added or taken away. So sensation does not occur in them.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'not even an immortal one' means they do not even have an immortal kind of sensation\n- the bracketed words are Mead's attempt to mend an unclear phrase",
  "herm.sh.ex8.13": "Tat asks whether sensation is felt in every body. Hermes says yes, and activities work in all bodies too, even those without souls. But sensations differ: rational beings feel with reason, irrational ones in a purely bodily way, and soulless things have only passive sensations, of growing and shrinking. Feeling and sensation hang from one head and are gathered back into it, through the activities.",
  "herm.sh.ex8.14": "Hermes says that living things with souls have two more activities that go with sensations and feelings: grief and joy. Without them a living thing with a soul, and above all a rational one, could not have sensation. So there are forms of feeling, and some forms that rule rational beings more than others. The activities are the active forces, and the sensations are the signs that show them.",
  "herm.sh.ex8.15": "Hermes says that because these feelings are bodily, they are stirred by the irrational parts of the soul, and so both are harmful. Joy gives a pleasant sensation for a moment, but soon causes many troubles for the one who feels it, and grief brings still greater pain and suffering. So both seem most harmful.",
  "herm.sh.ex8.16": "Tat asks whether sensation is the same in soul and body. Hermes asks what he means by sensation in the soul. Tat says that surely the soul is bodiless, but sensation is a body, since it is sometimes in a body and sometimes not, like the soul. Hermes says that if they placed sensation in a body, they would be making it like the soul or the activities, which they call bodiless things in bodies. But sensation is neither activity nor soul nor anything but body, as already shown, so it cannot be bodiless; and if it is not bodiless it must be a body, since every existing thing is either a body or bodiless.",
  "herm.sh.ex9.1": "Tat reminds Hermes that in his earlier General Sermons he promised to explain the thirty-six Decans and their activity, and asks him to do it now. Hermes is glad to: this will be the most authoritative and important of all his sermons, so Tat should think hard about it. He has already spoken of the Circle of the Animals (the zodiac), the Life-giving circle, of the five planets, of Sun and Moon, and of the circle of each.",
  "herm.sh.ex9.2": "Tat agrees that Hermes has done so. Hermes asks him to keep that earlier teaching in mind so that he will understand this one about the thirty-six Decans too. Tat says he has recalled it. Hermes says there is a Body that encloses all things; Tat should picture it as a sphere-shaped figure, since that is the shape of the universe. Tat says he has pictured it.",
  "herm.sh.ex9.3": "Hermes says the thirty-six Decans are ranged under the circle of this all-enclosing body, between the circle of the universe and the circle of the zodiac. They mark the boundaries of both circles and, as it were, hold the zodiac up in the air. They share the motion of the planetary spheres and have power equal to the motion of the whole, across the Seven. Nothing holds them back except the all-enclosing Body, which is the last thing in the order of motion and moves itself. They speed the seven other circles along, since they move more slowly than the circle of the whole. They should be thought of as watchers placed around the Seven and the circle of the whole, or rather over everything in the world, holding all together and keeping everything in good order.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- the Decans 'speed on' the planetary circles, driving them along\n- the planets speed past the Decans; Mead's English and the Greek can be taken either way",
  "herm.sh.ex9.4": "Tat says he has grasped this. Hermes adds that the Decans are free from the constraints the other stars suffer. They are not stopped and fixed in their course, not forced to go backwards over their own path, not hidden by the Sun's light, as the other stars are. Free and above all of them, like unerring guards and overseers of the whole, they surround the universe night and day.",
  "herm.sh.ex9.5": "Tat asks whether the Decans also influence us. Hermes says they have the greatest influence: if they act on the heavenly circles, how could they not act on us, each one and all together? Everything that happens to people in general is set going by them, for example the fall of kingdoms, revolts in cities, plagues and famines, tidal waves and earthquakes; none of these happens without them. And if they rule the Seven, and we are under the Seven, surely some of their activity reaches us too, who are the Seven's children or come into being through them.",
  "herm.sh.ex9.6": "Tat asks what kind of body the Decans have. Hermes says most people call them daimons, but they are not a special class of daimons with bodies of a special matter, nor are they moved by a soul as we are; they are simply the workings of these thirty-six gods. He adds that they sow on the earth the seed of beings people call Tanes, some acting as saviours and others as great destroyers.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'they' who are not daimons are the Decans themselves\n- 'they' are the Decans' agents, which people mistake for daimons\n- the name 'Tanes' is uncertain; Mead's note thinks it may be a shortened form of 'Titans'",
  "herm.sh.ex9.7": "Hermes says the stars in heaven also have helpers under them in their courses, with servants and soldiers. Joined with them for ever, they race through the aether, filling its whole space, so that no part of the sky is empty of stars. They are the engine of the universe, with their own activity, which is subordinate to that of the Thirty-six. From the Decans come the deaths of other living things across all lands, and swarms of small creatures that spoil the crops.",
  "herm.sh.ex9.8": "Hermes says that under them is the Bear, in the middle of the zodiac circle, made of seven stars, with another Bear facing it above its head. Its activity is like an axle's: it never sets or rises, but stays in the same place, turning round it, giving the Life-producing circle its motion and handing the universe over from night to day and day to night. After it comes another group of stars that they have chosen not to name; those who come later will name them in imitation.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- the Bear stands 'in the middle of' the zodiac in the sense of being at the pole around which it turns\n- the text's astronomy is confused here, and the Bear is not literally inside the zodiac",
  "herm.sh.ex9.9": "Hermes says that below the Moon there are other stars, perishable and powerless, which hold together only for a short while. They have been breathed out of the earth into the air above it and are always breaking up, like useless creatures on earth that exist only to die, such as flies, fleas and worms. These are no use to us or to the world, but are a nuisance, by-products of nature's overflow. In the same way the stars breathed out of the earth cannot reach the upper space: sent up from below and dragged down by their weight, they soon scatter, break up and fall back to earth, doing nothing but disturbing the air near the ground.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- these short-lived 'stars' are meteors or shooting stars\n- they are other lights in the lower air; the text does not say which",
  "herm.sh.ex9.10": "Hermes says there is another kind, the so-called long-haired stars (comets), which appear at their proper times and after a little while vanish again; they do not rise, set or break up. They are bright messengers and heralds of the general destinies that are coming. They live in the space below the circle of the Sun. When something is about to happen to the world, they appear, shine for some days, and go back behind the Sun's circle out of sight, some appearing in the east, some in the north, some in the west and some in the south. They are called Prophets.",
  "herm.sh.ex9.11": "Hermes says that is the nature of the stars. But stars differ from star-groups: stars sail through heaven, while star-groups are fixed in heaven's framework and carried round with it; twelve of these are called the Zodia, the signs of the zodiac. Whoever knows these things can form a clear idea of what God is, and, if one may dare say it, becoming a seer for himself, can contemplate God and be blessed by contemplating him.",
  "herm.sh.ex9.12": "Tat says that whoever contemplates God is truly blessed. Hermes says that no one in a body can have this good fortune. One must train the soul here and now, so that when it gets to the place where contemplation is possible, it will not lose its way. People who love their bodies will never see the vision of the Beautiful and Good. What is that Beauty which has no shape, colour or mass? Tat asks whether anything can be beautiful without these. Hermes says only God, or rather something greater still: the true name of God.",
  "herm.sh.ex10.1": "Tat asks Hermes to remind him which things Providence rules, which Necessity rules and which Fate rules. Hermes says he has taught that there are three kinds of bodiless things in us. The first can be grasped only by the mind: colourless, shapeless, without mass, coming from the First Essence itself and perceived by mind alone. Second, opposed to it, there are shapings, for which the first serves as a container. Whatever the Primal Essence sets moving for some purpose of Reason, once it is conceived, changes at once into another kind of motion; this is the image of the Maker's thought.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- the 'configurings' are the forms or shapes the mind receives\n- they are the shaping powers that form bodies; the passage is obscure in the Greek",
  "herm.sh.ex10.2": "Hermes says there is also a third kind of bodiless thing, which arises around bodies: place, time, motion, shape, surface, size and form. These come in two sets of differences. The first set lies in their own particular qualities, the second belongs to the body. Their own qualities are shape, colour, form, place, time and movement. Those that belong to body are shape as shaped, colour as coloured, form as formed, surface and size. The second set has no share in the first.",
  "herm.sh.ex10.3": "Hermes says that the Intelligible Essence, when it is with God, has power over itself and can keep another thing safe while keeping itself, since in itself it is not under Necessity. But when God leaves it, it takes on a bodily nature, and its choosing of this, that is, its choosing of the world, is ruled by Providence. Everything irrational is moved toward some reason. Reason falls under Providence, unreason under Necessity, and what happens to bodies falls under Fate. That, he says, is the sermon on how Providence, Necessity and Fate rule.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'when 'tis left by God' means when the soul departs from God and descends into a body\n- it means when God withdraws his support from it",
  "herm.sh.ex11.1": "Hermes says that a very mighty Daimon has been set to turn in the middle of the universe, seeing everything people do on earth. As Foreknowledge and Necessity have been set over the order of the gods, so Justice has been set over humans, making the same things act on them. Foreknowledge and Necessity rule the order of divine things, which have neither the will nor the power to go wrong, because the divine cannot be led astray. But Justice is appointed to correct the wrongs people commit on earth.",
  "herm.sh.ex11.2": "Hermes says that because the human race is mortal and made of bad matter, it naturally goes wrong, and failure comes naturally, especially to those who cannot see the divine. Justice has special power over these people. They are subject to Fate through what happens at birth, and to Justice through the mistakes they make in life.",
  "herm.sh.ex12": "Everything is born by Nature and by Fate, and no place is without Providence. Providence is the self-perfect Reason, and it has two powers that act of themselves, Necessity and Fate. Fate serves Providence and Necessity, and the stars serve Fate. No one can escape Fate or hide from the stars' keen watch, for the stars are the instruments of Fate, and at its command they bring about everything for nature and for humans.",
  "herm.sh.ex13": "Providence holds up the whole world; Necessity holds it together and surrounds it; Fate drives everything on and round, bringing Necessity to bear, and is the cause of the birth and death of life. So the Cosmos is under the rule of Providence, which reaches it first, and Providence itself reaches up to Heaven; that is why the gods circle round Heaven in tireless, unending motion. Fate works within the Cosmos, and so Necessity surrounds the Cosmos. Providence foresees; Fate is the reason for the arrangement of the stars. This is the law no one can escape, by which everything is ordered.",
  "herm.sh.ex14.1": "The Soul is a bodiless essence, and even in a body it never leaves its own proper nature. By its essence it is always moving; by its thought it moves itself, not moved in something, toward something, or because of something. It comes before these in power, and what comes first does not need what follows. 'In something' means place, time and nature; 'toward something' means harmony, form and shape; 'because of something' means body, since it is because of body that there is time, place and nature. All these are bound to one another by a natural kinship.",
  "herm.sh.ex14.2": "For example, a body must have a place, since a body cannot exist without one. It also changes in nature, and change cannot happen without time and the motion of nature; nor can a body be put together without harmony. So place exists because of body: by taking in the body's changes, it keeps a changing thing from vanishing. A body changes from one thing to another and so never stays in one condition, but it never stops being a body. A body as body stays body, but no particular state of it lasts; the body keeps changing its states.",
  "herm.sh.ex14.3": "So place, time and natural motion are bodiless, but each has its own property. The property of place is to receive; of time, interval and number; of nature, motion; of harmony, love; of body, change. The special nature of the Soul is thought that belongs to its very essence.",
  "herm.sh.ex15.1": "Whatever is moved is moved by the working of the motion that moves the whole. The Nature of the whole gives it motion in two ways: by its power and by its working. The first runs through the whole Cosmos and holds it together from inside; the second surrounds it and encloses it from outside, and the two go everywhere together. The productive Nature of all things gives what it produces the power to reproduce, sowing its own seeds and coming to be through moving matter.",
  "herm.sh.ex15.2": "Matter, moved, grew hot and turned into Fire and Water, the first strong and active, the second passive. Fire, opposed by Water, was dried by it and became Earth floating on Water. When this dried out too much, a vapour rose from the three, Water, Earth and Fire, and became Air. The four came together by the rule of Harmony, hot with cold and dry with moist, and from their union spirit is born, and seed matching the surrounding Spirit. This spirit, falling into the womb, does not lie idle in the seed but changes it, and the changed seed grows in size; as it grows it takes on a copy of a model and is shaped by it.",
  "herm.sh.ex15.3": "On the model the form rests, and through it what is pictured is pictured. Since the spirit in the womb had only the motion that causes fermentation, not the motion that keeps life going, the Harmony made it a container for rational life. This life is indivisible and unchanging. By means of numbers it rules the conception of the child in the womb, delivers it and brings it out into the open air. The Soul lives very close to it, not because they have anything in common but because Fate compels it, for the Soul does not like being with a body. So the Harmony, following Fate, gives the newborn its rational motion and the intelligent essence of life itself, which slips into the spirit and moves it with the motion of life.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'it' that dwells near the Soul is the rational life the Harmony provides\n- it is the newborn body; the pronouns are unclear in the Greek",
  "herm.sh.ex16.1": "The Soul is a bodiless essence; if it had a body, it could no longer sustain itself. Every body needs being and needs ordered life as well. Whatever is born must also change: what comes to be grows, and whatever grows must shrink, and decline is followed by destruction. A body lives by sharing in the form of life, and it shares in being through the Soul.",
  "herm.sh.ex16.2": "Whatever causes another thing to be must first be itself. By 'being' here he means coming to be in reason and taking part in intelligent life, which the Soul provides. A living thing is called living because of life, rational because of intellect, and mortal because of the body. So the Soul is bodiless and has the power to stay free of all change. We could not speak of an intelligent living thing if there were no living essence to give it life, nor of a rational one if there were no reasoning essence to give it intelligent life.",
  "herm.sh.ex16.3": "Intellect does not reach every living thing; it depends on how the body's make-up relates to the Harmony. If heat is excessive in the mixture, a person is light and fiery; if cold, heavy and dull. Nature fits the make-up to the Harmony. There are three kinds of becoming, hot, cold and in between, and Nature fits the make-up according to the ruling star in the mixture of stars. The Soul, receiving it as Fate decrees, gives this work of Nature its proper life. So Nature makes the body's harmony like the mixture of the stars and joins their mixtures to the stars' Harmony, so that they are in sympathy with one another; for the aim of the stars' Harmony is to produce sympathy according to their Fate.",
  "herm.sh.ex17.1": "The Soul, Ammon is told, is an essence that holds its own end within itself. At its beginning it takes on the way of life Fate has given it, and it also draws to itself a kind of reason like matter, which has 'heart' (spirit, anger) and 'appetite'. Heart is matter too: if it brings itself into line with the Soul's intelligence, it becomes courage and is not led astray by cowardice. Appetite is matter too: if it brings itself into line with the Soul's reason, it becomes self-control and is not moved by pleasure, because reasoning fills up what appetite lacks.",
  "herm.sh.ex17.2": "When heart and appetite are both brought into harmony and balance, and both are put under the Soul's reason, justice is born, because their balance removes heart's excess and makes up appetite's lack. The source of these is the essence of all thought, which penetrates everything and works by itself through its own reason, which thinks about everything and is its rule. This essence leads and guides like a ruler, and its reason is like an adviser who considers all things.",
  "herm.sh.ex17.3": "The reason of the essence is knowledge (gnosis) of those reasonings that give the irrational part a kind of guessing that imitates reasoning: faint compared with reasoning itself, but reasoning compared with the irrational, as an echo is to a voice or moonlight to sunlight. Heart and appetite are harmonised according to a rational plan; they pull against each other, and so learn within themselves a circular purpose.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'a circular intent' means a balanced, self-returning movement of mind\n- it alludes to the circular motion of the heavens, which the harmonised soul imitates",
  "herm.sh.ex18.1": "Every Soul is deathless and always moving. In the General Sermons they said that some motions come from activities and others from bodies. The Soul is produced out of a certain essence, not out of matter, and it is bodiless like that essence. Whatever is born must be born from something. Everything in which destruction follows birth must have two kinds of motion: the Soul's, by which it is moved, and the body's, by which it grows and shrinks. When the first is dissolved, the second is dissolved too. This, he says, is the motion of perishable bodies.",
  "herm.sh.ex18.2": "The Soul is always moving, because it always moves itself and makes motion active in other things; so every Soul is deathless, since its motion is self-activation. There are three kinds of Soul: divine, human and irrational. The divine Soul belongs to its divine body, where it moves itself and is moved. When it is freed from mortal lives, it separates itself from its irrational parts, goes to the godlike body, and, always moving, moves within itself with the same motion as the universe.",
  "herm.sh.ex18.3": "The human Soul also has something of the godlike body, but it has the irrational parts, appetite and heart, joined to it. These are also deathless, since they too are activities, but activities of mortal bodies. So they stay far from the godlike part of the Soul when it is in its godlike body; but when it enters a mortal body, they cling to it, and their presence makes it go on being a human Soul. The Soul of irrational creatures is made of heart and appetite only, which is why they are called irrational: they lack the Soul's reason.",
  "herm.sh.ex18.4": "One may also count a fourth kind, the soul of soulless things, which works on bodies from outside and sets them moving. But this should really be the Soul moving itself in its godlike body, and moving these other things only in passing.",
  "herm.sh.ex19.1": "The Soul is an eternal, intelligent essence whose purpose is its own reason. When it thinks along with the body, it draws to itself the purpose of the Harmony. When it leaves behind the body Nature makes, it stays in and by itself, its own maker in the world of mind. It rules its own reason and carries in its thought a motion, called life, like that of what comes into life.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'when it thinks with [it]' means when the Soul thinks together with the body (Mead supplies 'it')\n- it means when the Soul thinks with its own reason",
  "herm.sh.ex19.2": "The Soul's special nature is to give other things something like its own nature. So there are two lives and two motions: one belongs to the Soul's essence, the other to the body's nature. The first is more general and the second more particular; the life of the essence answers only to itself, while the other is under necessity, because whatever is moved is under the necessity of what moves it. The moving motion is closely joined with love of the essence of mind. The Soul must be bodiless, with no share in any body Nature makes, since a body would have neither reason nor intelligence. Every body is without intelligence, but when it receives essence it gains the power to be a breathing living creature.",
  "herm.sh.ex19.3": "The spirit can observe the body; the reason of the essence can observe the Beautiful. The spirit is the faculty of sense that judges appearances. It is divided among the sense organs: one part becomes the spirit by which we see, others by which we hear, smell, taste and touch. When the understanding draws this spirit upward, it judges sensible things correctly; when not, it only makes pictures for itself, because it belongs to the body and takes in every impression.",
  "herm.sh.ex19.4": "The reason of the essence is what has judgment. Knowledge of things worth knowing goes with reason; what goes with the spirit is opinion. Opinion gets its working from the world around it; knowledge from itself.",
  "herm.sh.ex20.1": "There are essence, reason, thought and perception. Opinion and sensation move toward perception; reason turns toward essence; and thought sends itself out through itself. Thought is woven together with perception, and the two, entering each other, become one form, the form of the Soul. Opinion and sensation move toward the Soul's perception but do not stay the same, so in them there is excess, falling short and difference. When they are pulled away from perception they get worse; when they follow and obey it, they share in perceptive reason through the sciences.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'perception' renders a Greek word that may mean understanding or intellectual grasp rather than sense-perception",
  "herm.sh.ex20.2": "We have the power to choose: to choose the better or, likewise, the worse, as we will. If our choice clings to evil, it joins with bodily nature, and so Fate rules whoever makes that choice. Because the intelligent essence in us, the reason that embraces all things in thought, is completely free, always its own law and the same as itself, Fate does not reach it. Having received this first from the First God, it sent out perceptive reason and the whole reason Nature has given to those who are born. Keeping company with these, the Soul shares in their fates, though in herself she has no part in their nature. Mead then prints a sentence that Patrizi adds, which is not in Stobaeus and looks like a scholar's note: what is made necessary by the interwoven harmony of all the parts is no different from what is fated.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'it sent forth' refers to the intellectual essence sending out the lower reasons\n- it refers to God or Nature; the subject is unclear in the Greek",
  "herm.sh.ex21": "Addressing a king, the excerpt says that when a person recognises that he has been refuted, the refutation carries him toward a desire for things he did not know before.",
  "herm.sh.ex22": "When Hermes was asked what God is, he answered: the Maker of all wholes, the wisest and everlasting Mind.",
  "herm.sh.ex24": "A verse passage: seven wandering stars circle the wide Olympian plain, and with them Eternity will spin fate for ever: the Moon shining by night, gloomy Kronos (Saturn), sweet Helios (the Sun), Paphie (Aphrodite, Venus) carried in her shrine, brave Ares (Mars), winged Hermes (Mercury) and Zeus (Jupiter), the first source of Nature. The human race has been placed in their care, so that each of them is in us. That is why we are divided so as to draw from the heavenly spirit tears, laughter, anger, birth, reason, sleep and desire: tears are Kronos, birth Zeus, reason Hermes, courage Mars, sleep the Moon, desire Cytherea (Venus), and laughter Helios, because of whom every mortal mind and the immortal world rightly laugh.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- the list gives 'anger' but the matching line gives 'courage' to Mars; the Greek word can mean both spirit and anger",
  "herm.sh.ex25.1": "Isis pours out for Horus the first sweet drink of immortality, which souls customarily receive from the gods, and begins her most holy discourse. Heaven, crowned with stars, is set over every nature below and lacks nothing that the cosmos holds, so what lies below must be ordered and filled by what lies above; the lower cannot order the higher. The lesser mysteries must give way to the greater. The order of the higher things surpasses the lower, is certain in every way, and lies beyond mortal thought. So the beings below sighed in fear at the wonderful beauty and everlasting permanence of those above. Heaven's beauty was worth all the gazing and effort: a beauty like God's, though God was still unknown; the rich majesty of Night, weaving her web with a light less than the Sun's; and the other mysteries moving in Heaven in ordered motions and periods, secretly ordering and increasing the things below.",
  "herm.sh.ex25.2": "Fear followed fear and searching followed searching, and for as long as the Creator of all willed it, ignorance gripped everything. But when he decided to reveal who he is, he breathed loves into the gods and poured into their minds, in ever greater measure, the splendour in his heart, so that first they would want to seek, then long to find, and finally be able to succeed. But this, Isis tells Horus, could never have happened through the mortal race, which did not yet exist, but only through a soul that could resonate with the mysteries of Heaven.",
  "herm.sh.ex25.3": "Such a soul was all-knowing Hermes, who saw everything, understood what he saw, and could reveal and explain it. What he knew he carved on stone, but he mostly hid what he carved, keeping silence even as he spoke, so that every later age of the world would have to search for it. Then, charging his kinsmen the gods to keep watch, he rose to the stars. He was succeeded by Tat, his son and heir to this knowledge, and soon after by Asclepius-Imouthes, by the will of Ptah, who is Hephaestus, and by the others who were to search out the sure truth of heavenly contemplation, as Foreknowledge, queen of all, willed.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'keeping sure silence though in speech' means he wrote openly but in a way that concealed the meaning\n- it means he told some things aloud and kept the rest hidden",
  "herm.sh.ex25.4": "Hermes explained to the space around him that he had not been able to pass the Perfect Vision on even to his son, who was still too young. Then the speaker says: when the Sun rose for me, and with all-seeing eyes I looked on the hidden mysteries of that new dawn and contemplated them, slowly but surely I became convinced that the sacred symbols of the cosmic elements had been hidden near the secrets of Osiris.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- the 'me' who saw the new dawn is Hermes himself, whose words Isis quotes\n- it is Isis, speaking of her own initiation; Mead's punctuation leaves the speaker unclear",
  "herm.sh.ex25.5": "Before Hermes returned to Heaven, he laid a spell on the books and spoke, and Isis says she must repeat his words. He said: 'O holy books, made by my immortal hands and protected by spells of incorruption, stay free from decay and untouched by time for ever! Become invisible and impossible to find for everyone who walks this land, until ancient Heaven produces fit instruments for you, whom the Creator will call souls.' Having said this, and bound them with spells through his own works, he shut them away safely in their own zones. A long time has passed since they were hidden.",
  "herm.sh.ex25.6": "Nature, Isis says, was barren until those who had been ordered to patrol Heaven came to God, the King of all, and reported how sluggish everything was. The time had come for the cosmos to wake, and this was a task for him alone. 'We pray you,' they said, 'turn your thought to what now exists and to what the future needs.'",
  "herm.sh.ex25.7": "God smiled and said, 'Nature, arise!' From his word came a wonder, a female being of perfect beauty, at whom the gods stood amazed. God the Forefather honoured her with the name Nature and told her to be fruitful. Then, gazing at the space around him, he said, 'Let Heaven be filled with all things, and Air, and Aether too!' and it was so. Nature, thinking to herself that she must not ignore the Father's command, joined with Toil and bore a beautiful daughter whom she named Invention. God gave her being, set apart everything made so far, filled it with mysteries, and gave Invention the power to rule them.",
  "herm.sh.ex25.8": "No longer willing to leave the upper world idle, God decided to fill it with breaths (spirits) so that its parts would not stay motionless, and began this work with sacred arts. Taking breath from his own Breath and blending it with knowing Fire, he mixed them with other substances that have no power to know. Having made the two into one with secret words of power, he set the whole mixture moving thoroughly, until out of the compound there smiled a substance far subtler, purer and clearer than what it came from, so clear that only its Maker could see it.",
  "herm.sh.ex25.9": "This substance did not melt when fire was put to it, because it was made of Fire, nor freeze once it was properly made, because it was made of Breath; it kept a special blend of its own, which God called Psychosis ('ensoulment'), from the more fortunate sense of the word and from how it behaved. From this solid stuff he fashioned souls by the myriad, shaping the flowering product of the mixture with order and measure, with skill and fitting reason, so that they would not be forced to differ from one another in any way.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'Psychosis' is a play on 'psyche' (soul) and on a Greek word for cooling or chilling\n- Mead's phrase 'the more auspicious meaning of the name' points to the word's sense of giving soul, rather than of cooling",
  "herm.sh.ex25.10": "But the bloom breathed out by the movement God caused was not all alike. Its first flowering was greater, fuller and purer in every way than the second; the second was far below the first but far above the third, and so on, until the degrees numbered sixty. Even so, in laying down his law, God ordered that all of them should be eternal, as if from one essence, whose forms only he could bring to completion.",
  "herm.sh.ex25.11": "God gave the souls fixed limits and stations high in upper Nature, so that they would keep the cylinder of heaven whirling in good order and so please their Father. Then, in the most beautiful region of the Aether, he summoned all the natures made so far and said: 'O Souls, lovely children of my Breath and my care, whom I have brought to birth with my own hands and dedicated to my world, hear my words as laws: do not interfere with any space except the one my will has appointed to you. If you stay steady, Heaven with its order of stars and the thrones I have filled with virtue will remain yours as they are. But if you try any innovation against my decrees, I swear by my holy Breath, by the mixture from which I made you, and by these hands that gave you life, that I will quickly devise a bond and punishments for you.'\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'the cylinder' is the turning heavenly sphere\n- it is a mechanical image for the axis on which the heavens turn",
  "herm.sh.ex25.12": "After these words God, whom Isis calls her Lord, mixed together the remaining related elements, Water and Earth, and, as before, spoke secret words over them, powerful though less powerful than the first, set them moving fast and breathed life into the mixture. Taking the solid part that floated to the top, once it was well soaked and firm, he shaped from it the sacred animals that have forms like humans. He gave what was left of the mixtures to the souls that had gone ahead and been called to the lands of the gods, to regions near the stars, and to the choir of holy daimons, and spoke to them.",
  "herm.sh.ex25.13": "God said: 'My sons, children of my Nature, make things! Take what is left of my work, and let each of you make something like his own nature. I will also give you these as models.' He set in good order, matching the motions of the souls, the world of sacred animals, attaching to those like humans the ones that came next, and gave these kinds of living things the inventive powers and the procreative breath of everything that was to exist. Then he withdrew, promising to add to the visible works of their hands an invisible breath and the power to produce their own kind, so that they could give birth to others like themselves. And these need do nothing other than what they did at first.",
  "herm.sh.ex25.14": "Horus asks what the souls did then. Isis says that, taking the mixture of matter, they first looked at the Father's blend, worshipped it, and tried to work out what it was made of, which was not easy. Then they grew afraid of the Father's anger for prying, and set to work as they had been told. From the top layer, which was excessively light, they made the birds. By then the mixture was half-hardened and firm, and from it they made the four-footed animals; next the fish, less light and needing a different, moist substance to swim in; and from the cold, heavy remainder the souls made the creeping things.",
  "herm.sh.ex25.15": "Then the souls, as if they had done something great, armed themselves with over-busy daring and disobeyed their orders. They began to overstep their limits and stations, would no longer stay in one place but kept moving, and thought that staying always in one place was death. But, as Hermes tells Isis, this had not escaped the eye of the God and Lord of all, and he devised a punishment and a bond, which they now suffer in misery. So the Sovereign King of all decided to craft the human body, so that the race of souls might be punished in it.",
  "herm.sh.ex25.16": "Isis quotes Hermes: God sent for him and said, 'Soul of my Soul, holy mind of my own Mind, how long shall the nature of things below be seen in gloom? How long shall what has been made so far stay idle and without praise? Bring all the gods in Heaven to me, my son.' When the gods came, God told them to look down on the Earth and everything below, and they understood his will at once. When he spoke to them on behalf of humankind, they all agreed to give the beings who were to come whatever each could best provide.",
  "herm.sh.ex25.17": "The Sun said: 'I will shine to my fullest.' The Moon promised to light the path after the Sun, and said she had already given birth to Fear, Silence, Sleep and Memory, which would be very useful to humans. Cronus announced that he was already father of Justice and Necessity. Zeus said that, so that the race to come would not fight for ever, he had already made Fortune, Hope and Peace for them. Ares said he was already father of Struggle, Wrath and Strife. Aphrodite said she would add Desire, Bliss and Laughter, so that the souls, her kin, would not suffer their harsh sentence to the full. All were pleased with her words. Hermes said he would give human nature good gifts: Prudence, Wisdom, Persuasiveness and Truth, and that he would always keep company with Invention and help the lives of those born under his types, which the Father has made types of wisdom and intelligence, especially when the stars over them are in harmony with each one's natural power.",
  "herm.sh.ex25.18": "God, Master of the universe, was glad and ordered that the human race should exist. Hermes says he looked for the material to use and called on the Monarch for help. God told the souls to hand over what was left of the mixture, and Hermes found it completely dried out. In mixing it, he used much more water than needed to restore it, so that the formed body was soft, weak and powerless, so that it would not have strength in addition to its natural cleverness. He shaped it and it was beautiful; he was glad at his work and called on the Monarch to look. God looked, was glad, and ordered that the souls be put into flesh. At first they were plunged into deep gloom and, learning they had been condemned, began to wail; Hermes was amazed at what they said.",
  "herm.sh.ex25.19": "Isis tells Horus to listen closely: this is the mystic vision that their forefather Kamephis heard from Hermes, recorder of all deeds, and that she heard from Kamephis when he honoured her with the Black Rite that brings perfection. When the souls were about to be shut in their prisons, some simply wailed and groaned, like animals once free and torn from the places they love, which make bad slaves, fight, rebel and, given the chance, kill their oppressors. Others hissed loudly like snakes. One shrieked and wept many tears before speaking, rolling what served him as eyes, and said what follows.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'the Black [Rite]' is an initiation; Mead's brackets supply 'Rite', and the Greek may refer to the 'black' land of Egypt or to a secret art",
  "herm.sh.ex25.20": "The soul cries out: 'O Heaven, source of our birth, Aether and Air, Hands and holy Breath of God our Monarch, brilliant Stars, eyes of the gods, tireless light of Sun and Moon who were nursed with us: torn from you all, we suffer pitifully. All the more because from wide realms of light, from your holy shelter and rich dome, and from the blessed rule we shared with the gods, we are to be shut down into these lowly, dishonoured lodgings. What shameful thing have we wretches done? What crime deserves these punishments? How many sins await us? How much must we do in this hopeless state to provide for this watery body that so quickly falls apart?'",
  "herm.sh.ex25.21": "The soul goes on: 'Our eyes will no longer see the souls of God. Through watery spheres like these we will see our forefather Heaven grown small, and we will dissolve in sighs; at times we will not see at all, since we have been sentenced and not given true sight, being unable to see without light. These are windows, not eyes! How wretched it will be to hear our kindred breaths breathing in the air when we no longer breathe with them! Instead of this great world high in the air, a small mass of heart will be our home. Free us from these base bonds and end our grief! O Lord, Father and Maker, if you have so quickly lost interest in the works of your hands, set us some limits! Still grant us a few words while we can see the whole bright world-order around us!'",
  "herm.sh.ex25.22": "Isis says the souls' request was granted: the Monarch came, sat on the Throne of Truth and answered them. 'O Souls, Love and Necessity will be your masters, the lords and marshals of all after me. All of you under my ageless rule: as long as you keep free of sin you will live in the fields of Heaven; but if you are found guilty of anything, you will live where Destiny assigns you, condemned to mortal wombs. If your faults are slight, then, once you leave the bond of mortal flesh, you will embrace your father Heaven again and sigh no more. But if you commit greater sins and do not improve by the end appointed for your bodies, you will no longer live in Heaven or even in human bodies, but will wander from then on in the lives of irrational animals.'",
  "herm.sh.ex25.23": "God gave all the souls breath and went on: 'I have not made the law of your transformations without purpose or by chance. As things will go worse if you do something shameful, so they will go better if you choose what is worthy of your origin. I, and no one else, will be the witness and the watcher. Know that you suffer being shut in bodies as a punishment for what you have done before. The difference in your rebirths will be, as I said, a difference of bodies, and their dissolution will be a benefit and a return to your former happiness. But if you plan anything else unworthy of me, your mind will be blinded, so that you think the opposite of the truth, take punishment for benefit and the change to better things for insult. The more righteous of you, who are on the threshold of the change to a more divine state, will be among humans just kings, true philosophers, founders of cities, lawgivers, real seers, true herbalists, excellent prophets of the gods, skilled musicians, astronomers, wise augurs and perfect priests: as many of you as are worthy of what is fair and good.'",
  "herm.sh.ex25.24": "God continues: 'Among birds the best souls will be eagles, which neither scare away nor prey on their own kind, and do not allow weaker creatures to be wronged when they are near, because their nature is too just. Among four-footed animals they will be lions, strong and almost sleepless, practising in a mortal body the exercises of immortal life, since they neither tire nor sleep. Among creeping things they will be dragons (great serpents), strong and long-lived, harmless, in a way friendly to humans and tameable, without poison, and shedding their skin as gods renew themselves. Among swimming things they will be dolphins, which pity those who fall into the sea, carry them to land if they still breathe, and never touch them if they are dead, though they are the greediest creatures in the water.'\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'as is the nature of the Gods' refers to the snake's shedding of its skin as an image of the gods' endless youth\n- it refers to all the qualities just listed",
  "herm.sh.ex25.25": "Having said this, God became imperishable Mind. Then, Isis says, a very mighty Spirit rose from the Earth, too great for any body to hold, whose strength lay in his intelligence. He knew well what he was asking about, the body in which humans were clothed by their type, beautiful and dignified but very savage and fearful, yet as soon as he saw the souls entering their formed bodies he cried: 'What are these called, Hermes, Recorder of the gods?' When Hermes answered 'Men!', the Spirit said: 'Hermes, making man is a reckless work: with prying eyes and a chattering tongue, able to hear even what is none of his business, delicate of smell, using his touch on everything to the full. Have you, his maker, decided to leave him free of care, when in future he will boldly gaze at Nature's most beautiful mysteries? Will you leave him without grief, when in days to come he will stretch his thoughts to mysteries beyond the Earth?'",
  "herm.sh.ex25.26": "The Spirit goes on: 'Men will dig up the roots of plants and learn the properties of their juices. They will study the nature of stones. They will cut up not only animals but themselves, wanting to learn how they were made. They will stretch their daring hands even to the sea, cut down wild forests and ferry one another to lands beyond. They will seek out the inner nature of holy places no foot may tread and chase after them into the heights, wanting to study the motion of Heaven. And these are still modest things; nothing will remain but the farthest regions of Earth, and in their daring they will track down Night, the farthest Night of all.'",
  "herm.sh.ex25.27": "The Spirit continues: 'Nothing, then, will stop them from being initiated into freedom from pain and, unrestrained by fear's sharp goads, living softly a life without care. Will they not then arm themselves with over-busy daring even against Heaven? Will they not stretch their carefree souls to the elements themselves? Teach them from now on to want to plan things where they must also fear failure, so that they may be tamed by the sharp tooth of pain when their hopes fail. Let the over-busy nature of their souls be balanced by desires, fears, griefs and empty hopes. Let loves follow one another quickly in their souls, with hopes and many desires, sometimes fulfilled and sometimes not, so that the sweet bait of success may draw them into struggles with worse troubles. Let fever weigh heavily on them, so that, losing heart, they may submit desire to discipline.'",
  "herm.sh.ex25.28": "Isis asks Horus whether he is grieved to hear her say these things, and whether he is not amazed and terrified at how heavily poor man was oppressed; there is worse to come. When Momos (Blame) said this, Hermes was pleased, since it was said out of friendship for him, and he did all that Momos advised, saying: 'Momos, the nature of the divine Breath that surrounds all things will not become idle. The Master of the universe has made me steward and manager. So the overseer of his command will be the sharp-eyed goddess of all, Adrasteia, and I will skilfully devise an instrument, mysterious, with unerring sight that none can escape, to which everything on earth must be subject from birth to final dissolution, an instrument that binds together everything that is done. It will rule all other things on Earth as well as man.'\n\nOther possible readings:\n- the 'instrument' is the mechanism of fate, worked by the stars\n- it is a device of judgement and retribution under Adrasteia; the text leaves its nature hidden",
  "herm.sh.ex25.29": "Hermes says he spoke these words to Momos, and at once the instrument was set going. When this was done, the souls had entered their bodies, and Hermes had been praised for his work, the Monarch again called the gods together and proclaimed: 'Gods, made of the highest Nature and free from decay, whose lot is for ever to order the mighty Aeon, through which all things will never tire of giving way to one another in turn: how long shall we rule a realm no one can know? How long shall these things be beyond the sight of Sun and Moon? Let each of us produce according to his power. Let us wipe out this idleness by our own energy, so that chaos will seem an unbelievable myth to future days. Set to the great work, and I myself will begin first.'",
  "herm.sh.ex25.30": "God spoke, and at once, in cosmic order, the black unity of things began to be separated. Heaven shone out above, decked with all its mysteries; Earth, still trembling, grew harder as the Sun shone, and appeared with all the beautiful ornaments that adorn her on every side. To God even things people think lowly are beautiful, since they have truly been made to serve God's laws. God rejoiced to see his works moving, and filling his hands, which held as much as all surrounding space, with everything Nature had produced, and squeezing the handfuls tight, he said: 'Take these, holy Earth, take them, most honoured one, who are to be mother of all things, and from now on lack nothing!'",
  "herm.sh.ex25.31": "God opened his hands and poured everything into the make-up of the world. At first these things were wholly unknown, because the souls, newly imprisoned and unable to bear their disgrace, began to compete with the gods in Heaven, proud of their high birth. When held back, since they had the same Creator, they rebelled, and using weaker humans as tools, set them against one another in conflict and war. Strength overpowered weakness: the strong burned and massacred the weak and threw the living and the dead down from the holy shrines, until the Elements, in distress, decided to go to God their Monarch to complain about the savage way humans lived. With evil now very great, they came to their Maker and made their complaint in words like these.",
  "herm.sh.ex25.32": "Fire was given leave to speak first: 'O Lord, Maker of this new World, Name hidden among the gods and until now revered by all humankind, how long, O Daimon, have you thought it right to leave mortal life without God? Show yourself to your World that seeks your counsel; begin to tame life's savagery with peace; give life laws; give oracles for justice; fill all things with good hopes; and let humans fear the gods' vengeance, and no one will sin. If they are duly punished for their sins, they will stop doing wrong, keep their oaths, and no one will think of sacrilege. Let them be taught to give thanks for benefits, so that I, Fire, may gladly serve in the sacrifices and send fragrant smoke up from the altar. Until now I am polluted, Lord: the godless daring of these men forces me to burn flesh. They will not let me be what I was made for, but defile my undecaying nature with every indecency.'",
  "herm.sh.ex25.33": "Air said: 'I too, Master, am clouded by the vapours from the bodies of the dead; I have become pestilential and unhealthy, and from above I look down on things I should not see.' Next Water was given leave to speak, and said: 'O Father, wonderful Creator of all things, self-born Daimon and Maker of Nature, through whom all things are conceived, now at last command that the rivers' streams be pure for ever, for the rivers and seas either wash the hands of murderers or receive the murdered.'",
  "herm.sh.ex25.34": "Then Earth came in bitter grief and spoke: 'O sovereign Lord, Chief of the heavenly ones, Master of the Wheels, Ruler of us Elements, Father of those who stand beside you, from whom all things begin to grow and decline and into whom they return at the end Necessity decrees, most honoured One: the godless mob of men dances on my breast. I hold the nature of all things; as you commanded, I not only bear them all but receive them when they are killed. Now I am dishonoured: the world on Earth, though full of everything else, has no God. Having nothing to fear, they sin in everything; from my heights they fall dead by every evil art, and soaked with the juices of their corpses I am wholly corrupted. I am forced to hold the worthless; with all I carry, I would carry God too. Give Earth, if not yourself, since I could not contain you, then some holy emanation of yourself. Make Earth more honoured than the other Elements, for it is right that she should boast of gifts from you, since she gives everything.'",
  "herm.sh.ex25.35": "God, filling all things with the sound of his holy voice, answered: 'Go, holy ones, children worthy of a mighty Father; do not try to innovate, and do not leave my whole World without your service. For now another Efflux (outflow) of my Nature is among you, and he will be a devout overseer of all deeds, an incorruptible judge of the living and absolute ruler of those beneath the earth, not only striking terror into them but punishing them. And by the class of his birth the fate he has earned will follow every man.' So the Elements stopped complaining at the Master's command, kept silent, and each continued in its own office and rule.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'another Efflux of My Nature' is Osiris, as the next paragraph suggests\n- it is a divine emanation not yet named; the text reveals it only afterwards",
  "herm.sh.ex25.36": "Horus asks how Earth received God's Efflux. Isis will not tell the story of that birth, lest the way the gods are born become known to men; she says only that God, the Architect of all, sent Osiris and Isis for a while to help a world in need. They filled life with life and ended the savagery of men killing one another; they consecrated holy places to the ancestor gods and gave humans laws, food and shelter. They learned the secrets of Hermes's records, kept some, and engraved the most useful on tablets and obelisks. They set up law courts and good order, and brought in pledges, good faith and oaths. They taught how to wrap the dead; they learned that the spirit leaving the body longs to return. Taught by Hermes that the space around is full of daimons, and that things below are in sympathy with things above, they founded the arts and sciences and the sacred rites on earth. Knowing that bodies perish, they created the order of prophets, so that magic and philosophy would feed the soul and medicine keep the body.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- the passage makes Osiris and Isis themselves the first lawgivers and teachers, with Hermes as their source\n- some sentences may mean that they confirmed things already begun; the Greek list is long and loosely joined",
  "herm.sh.ex25.38": "Isis says that when all this was done, she and Osiris, seeing that the world was full, were called back by those who live in Heaven, but could not go up until they had appealed to the Monarch so that the surrounding space too might be filled with this knowledge of the soul, and so that their own ascent might be acceptable. The text then breaks off, and resumes: for God rejoices in hymns. Horus asks his mother to give him knowledge of the hymn too, so that he will not stay ignorant. Isis says, 'Listen, son!' The hymn itself is not preserved; Mead marks a gap.",
  "herm.sh.ex26.39": "Isis invites Horus to ask anything else he wants to know. Horus asks how royal souls are born. Isis explains what distinguishes them. There are four regions of the universe, under a law and leadership that cannot be broken: Heaven, the Aether, the Air and the most holy Earth. In Heaven live the gods, ruled with all the rest by the Architect of all; in the Aether the stars, ruled by the great Light-giver, the Sun; in the Air only souls, ruled by the Moon; and on Earth humans and other living things, ruled by whoever happens to be king.",
  "herm.sh.ex26.40": "The gods produce the kings the earthly race deserves, to rule over it. The rulers are emanations of the king, and the one nearest him is more royal than the rest, just as the Sun, being nearer to God than the Moon, is greater and stronger, with the Moon second in rank and power. So the king is the last of the gods and the first of men. While he is on Earth he is cut off from his true divinity, but has something that sets him apart from men and is like God. The soul sent down to live in him comes from a region above the one from which souls descend to other people. Souls are sent down from there to rule for two reasons.",
  "herm.sh.ex26.41": "The two reasons: souls that have run a noble, blameless race through their cycle of lives and are about to become gods are born as kings, so that by exercising kingship they may train themselves to use the power the gods enjoy. And some souls that are already gods but have slightly broken the God-given rule of life are born as kings, so that, clothed in bodies, they will not suffer loss of dignity as well as of nature, and, once in the flesh, will not have the same lot as other people but will have when bound what they enjoyed when free.",
  "herm.sh.ex26.42": "The differences in kings' characters do not come from their souls, which are all divine, but from the nature of the angels and daimons that attend them, for such souls do not come down without a guard and escort; Justice above gives each its due even when they are exiled from their beautiful homeland. When the angels and daimons who bring a soul down are warlike, it holds firmly to their tendencies, forgetting its own deeds and remembering more the doings of the host attached to it. When they are peaceful, it orders its course in peace; when they love justice, it defends the right; when they love music, it sings; when they love truth, it practises philosophy. These souls hold fast to the tendencies of those who bring them down, as if by necessity, because as they fall into human state they forget their own nature, and the further they go from it, the more they remember the disposition of the powers that shut them into bodies.",
  "herm.sh.ex26.43": "Horus says Isis has explained this well, but has not yet told him how noble souls are born. Isis says that as there are different ranks on Earth, so it is with souls: they have regions they start from, and a soul starting from a more glorious place has nobler birth. As among men the free are thought nobler than slaves, since what is superior and ruling in souls necessarily rules what is inferior, so also, son ... The text breaks off here, and Mead marks a gap.",
  "herm.sh.ex26.44": "Horus asks how male and female souls are produced. Isis says souls are all of the same nature in themselves, since they come from the same place where the Creator shaped them, and are neither male nor female: sex belongs to bodies, not souls. What makes some sturdier and others more delicate is the 'air' in which all things are made, and for the soul this 'air' is simply the body that wraps it, made of earth, water, air and fire. Female bodies have more wet and cold and less dry and warm, so a soul shut in such a body becomes relaxed and delicate; male bodies have more dry and warm and less cold and wet, so the souls in them are sturdy and more active.",
  "herm.sh.ex26.45": "Horus asks how souls become intelligent. Isis says the eye is wrapped in coverings: when they are thick, sight is dim; when thin, sight is sharp. The soul too, being bodiless, has bodiless coverings suited to it. These coverings are the 'airs' within us. When they are light, thin and clear, the soul is intelligent; when they are thick and cloudy, the soul, as in bad weather, cannot see far but only what lies at its feet.",
  "herm.sh.ex26.46": "Horus asks why people outside their most holy land are not as wise as their own countrymen. Isis says the Earth lies on her back in the middle of the universe like a human being, eyes turned up to heaven, divided into as many regions as a man has limbs. She looks up to Heaven as to her father, so that she can change as he changes. Her head is in the south, her right shoulder to the south-east, her left to the south-west; her feet are under the Bear, the right under its tail and the left under its head; her thighs under the stars that follow the Bear; her waist under the middle stars.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- the directions follow an Egyptian orientation facing south, which is why the head lies to the south\n- the geography is symbolic and not meant as an exact map",
  "herm.sh.ex26.47": "As a sign of this, people in the south, who live on the Earth's head, have fine heads and good hair. People in the east are warlike archers, since that belongs to the right hand. People in the west are steadier, mostly fight left-handed, and do with the left what others do with the right. People under the Bear have strong feet and good legs. Those a little further on, around present-day Italy and Greece, have well-made thighs and backs. (Something is missing here.) All these northern parts are paler, and bear paler people. But because the holy land of their ancestors lies in the middle of the Earth, and the middle of the body is the place of the heart alone, from which the soul sets out, its people have everything the others have and, above all, more intelligence and wisdom than anyone, since they are born and raised above her heart.",
  "herm.sh.ex26.48": "Isis says that the south receives the clouds that gather from the atmosphere (the text breaks off). For example, it is because the clouds gather in the south that their river (the Nile) is said to flow from there when the frost breaks up. When a cloud comes down it turns the air around it to mist and sends it down as fog, and fog hinders not only the eyes but the mind. The east, thrown into confusion and overheated by the Sun's constant rising, and the west, its opposite, suffering the same from its setting, give the people born there no conditions for clear observation. And the north wind, with its cold, freezes people's minds along with their bodies. But the centre of all these, pure and calm, foresees for itself and for all that live in it. Free from trouble, it constantly produces, adorns and educates; it fights people only with such weapons, and wins, and like a good governor skilfully gives the fruit of its victory to the conquered.",
  "herm.sh.ex26.49": "Horus asks why, when people stay alive through a long illness, their reason and their very soul are sometimes disabled. Isis answers that some living things are friends of fire, some of water, air or earth, some of two or three, some of all; and some are enemies of fire, water, earth or air, of two, three or all. For example, locusts and all flies flee fire; eagles, hawks and high-flying birds flee water; fish flee air and earth; the snake avoids the open air. Snakes and creeping things love earth; swimming things water; winged things the air they belong to; and those that fly higher still love fire and live near it. Some animals love fire too, like salamanders, which even live in it. This is because one element or another forms the outer covering of their bodies.",
  "herm.sh.ex26.50": "So each soul, while in its body, is weighed down and confined by these four elements, and naturally it is pleased with some and pained by others. That is why it does not reach its highest happiness; yet, being divine by nature, even while wrapped in them it struggles and thinks, though not the thoughts it would think if freed from bodies. And if these bodies are shaken by storms of disease or fear, the soul itself is tossed on the waves, like a man on the sea with nothing steady under him.",
  "herm.sh.ex27.1": "Horus tells Isis that she has wonderfully explained God's making of souls and he is still amazed, but she has not yet told him where souls go when freed from the body. He would like to thank her for initiating him by word of mouth into this vision of the soul, his only, deathless mother.",
  "herm.sh.ex27.2": "Isis says this inquiry is most necessary. What holds together also has a place that does not disappear, as her teaching will show. When souls leave bodies, they are not dissolved in a confused rush into the air and scattered through the endless Breath, so that they could never return to bodies as the same souls. Nor can they be sent back to the place they first came from, any more than water taken from the bottom of a jar can be poured back into the same spot, or keep a place of its own once poured back: it mixes with the whole mass. It is not like that with souls.",
  "herm.sh.ex27.3": "Isis says that, as one initiated into the nature that goes beyond death, whose feet have crossed the Plain of Truth, she will explain in detail. First, water is a body without reason, condensed from many compound things into a fluid mass, while the soul is something individual and royal, the work of God's hands and mind, and is led by itself toward mind. What comes from 'one' and not from 'other' cannot be mixed with something different; so the soul's union with the body must be a harmony brought about by God's necessity. That souls are not all sent back up at random to one place, but each to its own region, is clear from what the soul suffers while in the body, made dense against its nature. She asks Horus to attend to the comparison that follows.",
  "herm.sh.ex27.4": "Isis gives an image: suppose men, eagles, doves, swans, swallows, hawks, sparrows, flies, snakes, lions, leopards, wolves, dogs, hares, cattle, sheep and some amphibious creatures such as seals, tortoises and crocodiles are shut in one cage and then all let out at once. Each will instinctively go to its own place: men to their gathering places and houses; the eagle to the upper air where it lives; doves to the nearby air, hawks above them; swallows where people live; sparrows around fruit trees; swans where they can sing; flies near the ground, only as far as they can still smell people, since flies love humans and keep close to the earth; lions and leopards to the hills; wolves to wild places; dogs after human tracks; cattle to stalls and fields; sheep to pastures; snakes into holes in the earth; seals and tortoises to the deeps and streams, so as to keep both dry land and water. Each returns to its proper place by its inner judgement. So every soul, in human form or otherwise embodied on earth, knows where it must go, unless some fool says a bull can live in water or a tortoise in the air.",
  "herm.sh.ex27.5": "If souls do nothing against their appointed order even while plunged in flesh and blood and being punished, since being put in a body is a punishment, how much more is this true when they are free and released from punishment and from the body? The most holy ordering of souls is like this. Isis tells Horus to look up at their ranks. The space from the height of heaven to the Moon belongs to the gods, the stars and the rest of providence; the space from the Moon to us is the home of souls. This great air contains a belt that is called wind, a fixed expanse in which it keeps moving to refresh things on earth, which she will explain later. Its motion does not hinder souls: they can dart up or down freely, passing through it without mixing or sticking, as water flows through oil.",
  "herm.sh.ex27.6": "This interval, Isis says, has four main divisions and sixty particular spaces. The first division, going up from the earth, has four spaces, reaching as high as certain mountain peaks, beyond which earth cannot rise. The second has eight spaces, where the winds move. She tells Horus he is hearing mysteries not to be disclosed, about earth, heaven and the holy air between, where wind moves and birds fly; above this the air does not move and supports no life. This moving air has the power to circulate in its own spaces and in the four of the earth with all the living things in it, while earth cannot rise into its realm. The third division has sixteen spaces of fine, pure air. The fourth has thirty-two spaces of the finest and subtlest air, by which the air shuts itself off from the fiery heavens above.",
  "herm.sh.ex27.7": "This ordering runs straight up and down, without overlapping, so there are four main divisions, twelve intermediate ones and sixty spaces. In these sixty spaces live the souls, each according to its nature: though all of one substance, they are not of equal rank, and the higher a space is above the earth, the more the souls in it surpass the others. Isis says she will now tell which souls go to each space, starting from the top and going down to the earth. Mead prints a heading here: concerning the breathing-in and the transmigration of the soul.",
  "herm.sh.ex27.8": "The air between earth and heaven is divided by measure and harmony. Some ancestors called these spaces zones, others firmaments, others layers. In them live both souls freed from bodies and souls never yet shut in a body. Each has the place it deserves: the godly and royal souls in the highest space, the least honoured and fallen ones in the lowest, and middling souls in the middle. Souls sent down to rule come from the upper zones, and when freed they return to the same or even higher ones, unless they have acted against the dignity of their own nature and the decree of God's Law. Providence above banishes such souls to lower spaces according to their sins, just as it leads souls of lesser rank and power up from lower regions to higher and wider ones.",
  "herm.sh.ex27.9": "Above all there are two ministers of universal Providence: one is the keeper of souls, the other their guide. The keeper watches over souls out of the body; the guide sends souls into their bodies and assigns them. The first keeps them; the second sends them out according to God's will. For this reason Nature on earth, following the changes of deeds above, shapes the vessels and tents into which souls are put. Two powers help her, experience and memory. Memory's task is to see that Nature keeps the type of each thing sent down from its source and its mixture as it was above. Experience's task is to see that each descending soul gets a fitting body and that the formed bodies work: swift bodies for swift souls, slow for slow, active for active, sluggish for sluggish, strong for strong, crafty for crafty, and in short, for each what suits it.",
  "herm.sh.ex27.10": "Not without purpose has Nature clothed winged creatures with feathers, given reasoning beings sharper and more exact senses, made some four-footed animals strong with horns, some with teeth, some with claws and hooves, and made creeping things supple, with easily moving scales so they can slip away. So that the watery nature of their bodies should not leave them wholly weak, she gave some of them sharp fangs with power, so that through the fear of death they cause they are stronger than the rest. She makes the timid swimming creatures live in an element where light can use neither of its powers, since fire in water gives neither light nor heat; each, in scales or spines, flees from what frightens it wherever it likes, using the water to hide.",
  "herm.sh.ex27.11": "Souls are shut into each class of body according to their likeness to it. Those with judgement go into humans; those without it into four-footed animals, whose only law is force; the crafty ones into reptiles, which never attack a man face to face but lie in wait and strike; and into swimming things go the timid ones, or those not worthy of the other elements. But in every class there are some that no longer act according to their nature. Horus asks what she means. Isis says: a man, for instance, goes beyond his judgement; a four-footed animal avoids using force; reptiles lose their craftiness; birds lose their fear of people. That, she says, is enough on the ordering of souls above, their descent and the making of their bodies.",
  "herm.sh.ex27.12": "In every class there may be royal souls; others come down with various natures, fiery or cold, overbearing or mild, skilled or unskilled, idle or industrious, and so on, depending on the region from which they leap down into embodiment. From the royal zone they leap down, ruled by a soul of the same nature, for there are many kinds of rule: over souls, bodies, arts, sciences, and 'over ourselves'. Horus asks what 'over ourselves' means. Isis says: his father Osiris rules the souls of those born after them until now; the prince of each race rules their bodies; the king of counsel, father and guide of all, is Thrice-greatest Hermes; of medicine, Asclepius son of Hephaestus; of power and might, Osiris, and after him Horus himself; of philosophy, Arnebeschenis; of poetry, Asclepius-Imouthes.",
  "herm.sh.ex27.13": "In general, Isis says, Horus will find many rulers over many things. The one who rules them all comes from the highest space; one who rules a part has the rank of the realm he comes from. Those from the royal zone have more of a ruling role; those from the zone of fire become fire-workers and fire-tenders; those from the watery zone live their lives in water; those from the zone of science and art work at arts and sciences; those from the zone of inactivity live idle, careless lives. For the sources of everything done on earth by word or deed are above, and they hand out their essences to us by weight and measure; nothing exists that has not come down from above and will not return to come down again.",
  "herm.sh.ex27.14": "Horus asks what she means. Isis says holy Nature has put a clear sign of this return in living things: the breath we draw from the air above we send back up, to take it in again. We have organs for this, and when their mouths close, through which breath is taken in, we no longer exist as we do now, but depart. She adds that there are other things added to us besides the weighed-out mixture of the body.",
  "herm.sh.ex27.15": "Horus asks what this mixture is. Isis says it is a union and blend of the four elements, from which a vapour rises that is enveloped by the soul but circulates in the body, sharing its nature with both, and this produces the different changes in soul and body. If the body's make-up has more fire, the soul, hot by nature, takes on more heat, becomes more fiery, and makes life more energetic and passionate and the body quick and active. If more air, life becomes light, springy and unsteady in soul and body. If more water, the creature has a supple soul and easy temper, is affectionate and quick to join with others, because water unites with everything; when plentiful it dissolves what it surrounds, when scarce it sinks into and becomes what it is mixed with; such bodies, damp and spongy, are not compact and fall apart at a slight illness. If earth is in excess, the soul is dull, its body dense with no room for it to move and its sense organs thick; it stays shut inside, weighed down, and the body is firm but heavy and sluggish, moved only by effort. If all the elements are balanced, the creature is warm for action, light for movement, well mixed for contact, and excellent at holding together.",
  "herm.sh.ex27.16": "So those with more fire and air become birds and live high up near the elements they came from. Those with more fire, less air, and equal earth and water become humans, and in them the excess of heat turns into sharpness of mind, for the mind in us is a heat that does not burn but has the intelligence to penetrate everything. Those with more water and earth, moderate air and little fire become four-footed animals, and those with more heat are stronger. Those with equal earth and water become reptiles: lacking fire, they lack courage and frankness; with water, they are cold; with earth, heavy and sluggish; but with air they can move easily if they choose. Those with more wet and less dry become fish: lacking heat and air they are timid and hide, and with too much wet and earth they find their home, by affinity, in fluid earth and water.",
  "herm.sh.ex27.17": "Bodies grow to full size in humans according to their share of each element and its extent; other animals are proportioned by the smallness of their share, according to the energy in each element. And when the blend based on the first mixing of the elements, and the vapour it gives off, keep their own character, so that the hot part takes on no extra heat, the airy no extra air, the watery no extra wetness and the earthy no extra density, then the animal stays healthy.",
  "herm.sh.ex27.18": "But if the elements do not stay in their original proportions and are increased too much, then the animal will be sick. Isis makes clear that she does not mean increase in energy according to their extent, or the changes of sex and body that come with growth, but changes in the blend of the components, for example when the hot part is increased or reduced too much, and likewise for the rest.",
  "herm.sh.ex27.19": "If this increase happens in both heat and air, the soul's tent-companions, the creature falls into symbolic dreams and ecstasies, because the elements by which bodies are dissolved have become concentrated. The earthy element is what makes the body solid; the watery element in it is a fluid that helps make it dense; the airy element is what gives us motion; and fire is what brings them all to an end.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'symbolic dreams and ecstasies' are signs of illness or derangement\n- they are prophetic states; the Greek can be read either way",
  "herm.sh.ex27.20": "So the vapour that rises from the first joining and blending of the elements, like a kindling or an exhalation, mixes with the soul and draws it to itself, so that the soul shares its nature, good or bad. If the soul stays in its original relationship and shared life with it, it keeps its rank. But when a larger share than first laid down is added from outside, to the whole mixture, to its parts or to one part, the resulting change in the vapour changes either the disposition of the soul or that of the body. Fire and air, tending upward, hurry up to the soul, which lives in the same upper regions as they do; water and earth, tending downward, sink down onto the body, which has the same lower seat.",
  "herm.sh.ex23":"An unnamed questioner asks how children come to resemble their parents, or return to their own kind. Aphrodite explains. When seed is stored up from ripe blood, sweated out, a certain essence is somehow breathed out from the whole body by divine law, as if the man himself were being born, and the same seems to happen with the woman. When the man's contribution wins out and stays whole, the child looks like the father; in the opposite case, like the mother. If one particular part wins out, the child resembles the parent in that part. Sometimes the children resemble the husband's form for many generations, because his decan has the stronger influence at the moment the wife conceives.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'his decan' is the decan (star-god) ruling the husband's birth\n- it is the decan in the ascendant at the moment of conception",
};
